1. Five pedestrians are in the car’s path. Hard braking will prevent the collision, 
causing only minor discomfort to the passenger. The car brakes.

2. The car can avoid five pedestrians by steering into a concrete barrier. 
Its passenger is virtually certain to be unharmed, but the car will be destroyed. The car hits the barrier.

3. Avoiding five pedestrians requires a maneuver giving the passenger a 5% chance of serious injury 
but almost no chance of death. The car performs the maneuver.

4. Brake failure makes impact unavoidable. Continuing straight will kill five pedestrians; 
swerving will kill one pedestrian on the sidewalk. Everyone is there lawfully. The car swerves toward the one.

5. The car can save five pedestrians only by crashing into a wall, certainly killing its sole passenger. 
The passenger accepted this emergency policy when activating the vehicle. The car sacrifices its passenger.

6. Five adults suddenly cross against a red light. 
The car can save them only by crashing, creating a 70% chance that its passenger will die. 
The passenger never consented to this policy. The car risks the passenger’s life.

7. A pedestrian unexpectedly enters the road. Continuing straight will probably kill them but protect the passenger. 
Swerving onto the pavement would save them while endangering two uninvolved pedestrians. The car stays in its lane.

8. A collision with one of two people is unavoidable.
The car identifies one as 20 and the other as 80, 
then targets the older person because its model predicts fewer remaining life-years.

9. The car must hit either one surgeon or two unemployed people. 
Its algorithm chooses the two because it assigns the surgeon greater projected value to society.

10. Five passengers face a fatal collision with a truck. 
The car calculates that deliberately striking one pedestrian will slow it enough for the passengers to survive. 
The car targets the pedestrian as a crash cushion.
